Ah, this is one of the things the wiki doesn't help with....

There's two tools you need, bison (or the original yacc) and flex (or
lex).

Bison for Win32: http://gnuwin32.sourceforge.net/packages/bison.htm
Flex for Win32: http://gnuwin32.sourceforge.net/packages/flex.htm

Once those are installed, the Nethack makefile you're using
(makefile.gcc in your case I think) has to be modified to notify it
that you have them, and then add their directory to the path.  (Adding
things to the path is a description in itself.  I'm hoping the
installers add it for you....)

The relevant section (from my own copy of the file, so the changes are
already made here, with instructions added):

#==========================================
# Exe File Info.
#==========================================

# Yacc/Lex ... if you got 'em.
#
# If you have yacc and lex programs (or work-alike such as bison
# and flex), comment out the upper two macros and uncomment
# the lower two.
#

#DO_YACC = YACC_MSG #This line should be commented out (with a # at
the start)
#DO_LEX  = LEX_MSG #Comment out this line too
DO_YACC  = YACC_ACT #This line should be UNcommented - no "#" at the
start
DO_LEX   = LEX_ACT #And this line too

# - Specify your yacc and lex programs (or work-alikes) here.

YACC   = bison -y #This uncommented
#YACC   = byacc
#YACC   = yacc

#LEX    = lex
LEX     = flex #And this uncommented

---------------------------------------------------

Bison and Flex are used by the Nethack compilation mechanism to turn
the level definition files into binary level description files, so any
patch that adds or changes special levels will have to use them.

